WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:07.669
Sometimes filmmakers hide obscure in-jokes
so subtly that it takes a few viewings to

00:00:07.669 --> 00:00:11.910
spot them, if you're able to spot them at
all. These hidden gags might just make you

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.850
want to watch these flicks all over again...

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.890
Shaun of the Dead's spoilerific beginning

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:20.940
The zombie horror-comedy Shaun of the Dead
opened the Cornetto Trilogy with a hilarious

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:25.240
look at how a British slacker and his pub-loving
friends handled the zombie apocalypse. But

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.119
if that lovable loser Shaun could speak Hindi,
he might have been clued in to the impending

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:32.079
zombie apocalypse when he went to the convenience
store.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:39.400
(radio broadcast playing)

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:42.690
The radio broadcast in the store actually
reveals that the dead are coming back to life

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:47.399
and attacking people. That joke is so well-hidden,
it's practically buried treasure.

00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:48.750
Nite-Owl saves the Waynes

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:53.500
The opening sequence of Watchmen has a pretty
awesome reference to a famous caped crusader.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.270
Check the frame featuring Nite Owl punching
a masked gunman outside of an opera house.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:02.629
The Gotham Opera House. Yes, it's Batman’s
origin! In the background is a rich couple,

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:06.960
clearly meant to be Bruce Wayne's parents,
Thomas and Martha. The opera bills read "Die

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.400
Fledermaus," German for "The Bat." And just
to make the joke a little more obvious, several

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.060
posters advertise the first issue of the Batman
comic book on the wall as well. Holy Easter

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.720
eggs, Batman!

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.060
Sherman and Peabody

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:22.890
There's a subtle reference to other time travelers
in Back to the Future, but you wouldn't get

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:27.700
the joke unless you stayed in the theater.
When Marty travels to 1955, he crashes the

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.020
DeLorean into the Peabody's family barn. The
members of the Peabody family poke their heads

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.650
into the barn, at which point that little
boy claims that Marty's time machine is actually

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:40.189
a UFO. If you watch through the credits, you
see that this boy's name is Sherman—Sherman

00:01:40.189 --> 00:01:45.119
Peabody, a reference to the cartoon time travelers
Mr. Peabody and Sherman.

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:46.880
"Quickly Sherman!"

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.530
Unlike the DeLorean crashing into the barn,
that joke flew right over most viewers' heads.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:52.960
Lord Farquaad

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.290
Even though he's in a movie for kids, Lord
Farquaad from Shrek was given that name to

00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:01.509
get a laugh out of the adults. If you didn't
catch the joke, here's a hint: try saying

00:02:01.509 --> 00:02:03.600
his name over and over.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.040
"I am Lord Farquaad."

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.010
"Lord Farquaad?"

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.340
Farquaad, Farquaad, Farquaad.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.459
That’s right, he's a complete f***wad.

00:02:14.459 --> 00:02:18.150
Imagine how Disney chief Michael Eisner felt,
considering the villain was reportedly based

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.489
on him...

00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:21.430
The Despicable...Lehman Brothers?

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:26.010
While the Financial Crisis of 2008 wasn't
funny, the creators of Despicable Me thought

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:29.480
a joke about it wouldn't hurt. If you have
eagle eyes, you probably noticed that when

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:33.180
Gru enters the Bank of Evil to get a loan,
there's a small sign that reads "Formerly

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.219
Lehman Brothers"—a jab at the global financial
services firm that went bankrupt and filed

00:02:37.219 --> 00:02:39.549
for Chapter 11 in 2008.

00:02:39.549 --> 00:02:41.809
Kevin Smith's Jaws References

00:02:41.809 --> 00:02:46.109
Director Kevin Smith is a film buff, and he
likes to show it. Want proof? He sneaks a

00:02:46.109 --> 00:02:49.829
boatload of Jaws references into his films.
Like the Clerks salsa shark.

00:02:49.829 --> 00:02:53.120
And Mallrats' characters, Brodie Bruce and
T.S. Quint.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.379
And Chasing Amy's Hooper X.

00:02:54.379 --> 00:02:58.019
He even takes it a step further in Chasing
Amy with an homage to the scene where Jaws'

00:02:58.019 --> 00:03:00.959
characters compare scars.

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:03.739
The twist? Instead of showing battle wounds,
Banky and Alyssa compare scars they received

00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:05.400
while romancing the ladies.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.709
"You're gonna need a bigger boat."

00:03:07.709 --> 00:03:10.790
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.989
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know which obscure movie jokes you think

00:03:13.989 --> 00:03:14.799
should've made the list...

